# Sign leaves through the one role, and nothing else.
#
# DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN AWS, whose copy of this file grants
# create/read/update/delete/list on `pki_private_issuer/*`. That covers the
# issuer configuration, the CA endpoints, every role and the tidy/revoke APIs --
# so anything holding a cert-manager token there could rotate the issuer, or add
# a role allowing any domain, rather than only mint a certificate for the domain
# this role permits. (It used to say "a leaked secret_id"; there is no secret_id
# any more, but a compromised ServiceAccount token buys the same reach, so the
# narrowness still earns its keep.)
#
# cert-manager's Vault issuer needs exactly two things at runtime:
#   - POST auth/jwt/<cluster>/login -- unauthenticated by definition (it presents
#     a projected ServiceAccount token), so it needs no policy. This was
#     auth/approle/login until the AppRole backend was removed; cert-manager now
#     authenticates with a cluster-issued JWT, and there is no secret_id to leak.
#   - POST <mount>/sign/<role> -- this rule
# Its readiness probe hits sys/health, which is unauthenticated by default.
#
# TEMPLATED, unlike the AWS copy: the path names the role, the role is what
# constrains the domain, and a `sign/*` wildcard would re-admit any role added
# later. Deriving both halves from the resources themselves (policies.tf) keeps
# THIS file correct across a rename of var.pki_mount_path or var.pki_organization.
#
# IT DOES NOT MAKE THE RENAME SAFE. The other half of the pair --
# security/gcp-0/cert-manager/openbao-clusterissuer.yaml -- still
# spells `pki_private_issuer/sign/ogenki` as a literal, and nothing in the repo
# compares the two. Rename either variable and the mount, role and policy all
# move together while the ClusterIssuer keeps pointing at the old path; with an
# exact-path policy there is no wildcard left to absorb it, so every issuance
# returns 403 with nothing to explain why. Closing that properly means
# publishing the mount and role into the GCP vars ConfigMap
# (opentofu/gcp/gke/configure/kubernetes.tf) and substituting them into the
# manifest.
path "${mount}/sign/${role}" {
  capabilities = ["create", "update"]
}
